Yes dear. For a business-facing Target/Analytics report, I would keep the glossary very simple and avoid Adobe-specific technical wording wherever possible.

Term	Business-oriented definition

Impressions	Number of times the Guided Investing offer/banner was displayed to users.
Clicks / Interactions	Number of tracked user interactions with the Guided Investing banner, such as clicking the configured banner link or CTA.
Click-Through Rate (CTR)	Percentage of banner impressions that resulted in a tracked click or interaction. It is calculated as Clicks ÷ Impressions × 100.
Unique Visitors	Number of distinct visitors who were identified during the selected reporting period.
Visits / Sessions	Number of individual browsing sessions during which users interacted with the site; one visitor can have multiple sessions.
Activity Map Link Instances	Number of tracked link interactions recorded for the selected banner/activity based on the applied Analytics segment or filter.
Offer Impression	A recorded instance of the Target offer being presented to the user.
Target Experience	The specific experience or variation assigned to a visitor as part of the Adobe Target activity.
Activity / Experience Segment	A reporting filter used to isolate users or interactions associated with the selected Target activity or experience.


CTR interpretation for this report

You can include this directly below the glossary:

> Click-Through Rate should be interpreted in relation to both impressions and clicks. A high number of impressions does not necessarily result in a high CTR. If the banner is displayed frequently but only a small proportion of those impressions lead to clicks, the CTR will remain low.



For the numbers visible in your report, this is very clear:

852 impressions → 20 tracked interactions → approximately 2.35% CTR

So a business-friendly observation would be:

> The Guided Investing banner received 852 impressions and 20 tracked interactions, resulting in a 2.35% click-through rate. This indicates that while the banner received reasonable visibility, only a relatively small proportion of displayed impressions resulted in user interaction.



One small wording recommendation, dear: for executives/business users, use “Tracked Banner Interactions” in the visible report instead of “Activity Map Link Instances” wherever possible. Keep the Adobe metric name in brackets if needed, because “Activity Map Link Instances” is technically meaningful to Analytics users but not intuitive to a business reader.
